// "Understand it" paragraphs, keyed by the sheet's # column.
// q: the question in plain words — what goes in, what comes out, what matters (never gives the approach away:
//    it also shows on the front of review cards)
// a: why the reference approach works — the intuition behind the steps
window.EXPLAIN = {
  // ── Arrays & Hashing ────────────────────────────────────────────
  3: { q: 'You get a list of numbers and only need a yes/no answer: does any number show up more than once? You don’t need to say which number repeats or how many times — the moment you find a single repeat, the answer is “yes”. If every value is different, it’s “no”. An empty or one-element list can’t have a repeat.',
    a: 'Checking every pair is slow because for each number you rescan everything before it. A hash set remembers every number seen so far and answers “have I seen this?” in constant time, so one left-to-right pass is enough: the first number that’s already in the set is a duplicate. You trade O(n) extra memory for O(n) time — sorting would save the memory but cost O(n log n).' },
  4: { q: 'Two strings are anagrams if you can rearrange the letters of one to get exactly the other — same letters, same number of each, order doesn’t matter. “listen” and “silent” are anagrams; “rat” and “car” aren’t. Strings of different lengths can never be anagrams.',
    a: 'Order doesn’t matter, only how many of each letter there are — so compare letter counts. Walk both strings together, adding 1 for each letter of s and subtracting 1 for each letter of t. If they use exactly the same letters, every add is cancelled by a subtract and all 26 counters end at zero. Any non-zero counter means one string has a letter the other is short of.' },
  5: { q: 'Given an array and a target, find the positions (indices) of two different elements that add up to the target. Exactly one such pair exists, and you can’t use the same element twice — though two different elements may hold the same value, like [3, 3] with target 6. Return their indices, not the values.',
    a: 'For each number x, the partner you need is fixed: target − x. Instead of searching for it, keep a map from every value already seen to its index. When you reach x, ask the map whether target − x was seen earlier — if yes, you have your pair. Checking before inserting x guarantees an element never pairs with itself, and one pass is enough because every pair is found when its second element arrives.' },
  6: { q: 'You get a list of words and must put words that are anagrams of each other into the same group — “eat”, “tea” and “ate” belong together, “bat” sits alone. Return all the groups; the order of groups and of words inside them doesn’t matter.',
    a: 'Anagrams look different but share something identical: their letter counts. Turn each word into a “signature” (its 26 letter counts, or the word sorted) and use that as a hash-map key. All anagrams produce the same signature, so they land in the same bucket automatically. One pass over the words builds every group.' },
  7: { q: 'Return the k numbers that appear most often in the array. For [1,1,1,2,2,3] with k = 2 the answer is [1, 2] because 1 appears three times and 2 twice. The answer is guaranteed to be unique, and any order is fine.',
    a: 'First count how often each number appears. Sorting by count would cost O(n log n), but counts can only be between 1 and n — so make an array of n + 1 buckets where bucket f holds the numbers that appear exactly f times. Walking the buckets from the highest frequency down hands you numbers in “most frequent first” order; stop once you’ve collected k. That’s bucket sort, O(n).' },
  8: { q: 'Build a new array where each position holds the product of every OTHER element — everything except the one at that index. For [1,2,3,4] the answer is [24,12,8,6]. The catch: you may not use division, and it should run in O(n).',
    a: 'The product of everything except i splits into two parts: everything to the LEFT of i times everything to the RIGHT of i. A forward pass with a running product fills each slot with its left part; a backward pass with another running product multiplies in the right part. No division is needed, so zeros cause no trouble, and the output array doubles as the storage.' },
  9: { q: 'Look at a partly filled 9×9 Sudoku board and say whether it’s valid so far: no digit may appear twice in any row, any column, or any of the nine 3×3 boxes. Empty cells (".") are ignored, and you don’t need to check whether the puzzle can actually be solved.',
    a: 'There are 27 rules (9 rows, 9 columns, 9 boxes) and each says “no repeats” — exactly what a set checks. Keep a seen-set per row, per column and per box, and scan the board once. The only trick is naming the box: rows and columns integer-divided by 3 give its coordinates, so box = (r / 3) · 3 + c / 3. A digit already in any of its three sets breaks a rule.' },
  10: { q: 'Expand an encoded string where k[text] means “text repeated k times”. “3[a]2[bc]” becomes “aaabcbc”. Brackets can nest — “3[a2[c]]” becomes “accaccacc” — and k can have several digits, like 12[x].',
    a: 'Nesting means you must pause the outer string while you build an inner one, then come back — that’s what a stack is for. On “[” you push the repeat count and the text built so far, and start fresh. On “]” the inner text is finished: pop the count and the outer text, and append the inner text that many times. Letters simply extend the current text. Each bracket level is saved and restored exactly once.' },
  11: { q: 'Find the length of the longest run of consecutive integers you can form from the array’s values, in any order. [100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2] contains 1, 2, 3, 4, so the answer is 4. Duplicates don’t extend a run, and the target is O(n) time — so sorting isn’t allowed.',
    a: 'Put every value in a hash set so “is x present?” is O(1). Then only start counting from numbers that BEGIN a run — those whose x − 1 is missing. From such a start, walk x + 1, x + 2, … while present. Every number is walked at most once as part of the single run it belongs to, so even with the inner loop the total work is O(n).' },

  // ── Two Pointers ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  13: { q: 'Decide whether a string reads the same forwards and backwards once you ignore everything except letters and digits, and ignore upper/lower case. “A man, a plan, a canal: Panama” counts as a palindrome. An empty string (after cleaning) is a palindrome too.',
    a: 'A palindrome mirrors around its middle, so compare from both ends at once. Put one pointer at the start and one at the end, skip any character that isn’t a letter or digit, and compare the two lowercased characters. One mismatch means it’s not a palindrome; if the pointers meet, every mirrored pair matched. No cleaned copy needed — O(1) extra space.' },
  14: { q: 'The array is already sorted in increasing order. Find two different numbers that add up to the target and return their positions — counted from 1, not 0. Exactly one answer exists, and you should use only O(1) extra space.',
    a: 'Start with the smallest and largest numbers. If their sum is too small, the only way to grow it is to replace the small one with something bigger — move the left pointer right. If it’s too big, move the right pointer left. Because the array is sorted, each move safely throws away a number that can’t be part of any answer, so you meet the pair in one pass.' },
  15: { q: 'Find every set of three numbers in the array that adds up to exactly zero. Each triplet must use three different positions, and the answer must not contain the same triplet twice — [-1, 0, 1] and [0, -1, 1] count as the same one.',
    a: 'Sort first. Then fix one number at a time and the problem becomes “find two numbers after it that add to minus it” — which is Two Sum II on a sorted range, solved with two pointers moving inward. Sorting also makes duplicates sit next to each other, so you avoid repeated triplets just by skipping a value equal to the one you just used. Total O(n²).' },
  16: { q: 'Each array value is the height of a vertical line. Pick two lines that, together with the x-axis, hold the most water. The water level is limited by the SHORTER line, and the width is the distance between them, so area = min(height) × distance.',
    a: 'Start with the widest container (both ends). To find anything better, you must lose width, so you need a taller limiting line. Moving the taller side inward can never help — the shorter side still caps the height while the width shrinks. So always move the shorter side. Each step discards a line that can’t be in a better answer, giving O(n).' },
  17: { q: 'The array is an elevation map of bars, each 1 unit wide. After rain, water collects in the dips between taller bars. Return how many units of water are trapped in total. Water can’t pile up at the very edges, because it would spill off.',
    a: 'Water above a bar is limited by the tallest bar on its left and the tallest on its right: min(leftMax, rightMax) − height. With two pointers moving inward, whichever side has the lower wall is the bottleneck — the other side is guaranteed to have something at least as tall. So you can settle the lower side’s water right away using only its own running max, then move that pointer in. One pass, O(1) space.' },

  // ── Sliding Window ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  19: { q: 'prices[i] is a stock’s price on day i. Choose one day to buy and a LATER day to sell to make the most profit. Return that profit — or 0 if prices only fall and no trade makes money. You make at most one buy and one sell.',
    a: 'If you sell today, the best day to have bought is the cheapest day before today. So walk the days once, remembering the lowest price so far; today’s best possible profit is today’s price minus that minimum. Keep the largest of those profits. One pass, two variables.' },
  20: { q: 'Find the length of the longest contiguous piece of the string (a substring, not a subsequence) in which no character appears twice. In “abcabcbb” the answer is 3 (“abc”); in “bbbbb” it’s 1.',
    a: 'Keep a window [left, right] that never contains a repeat, and grow it one character at a time. When the new character already appears inside the window, everything up to and including its previous position has to go — so jump left just past that earlier occurrence (remembered in a last-seen map). Never let left move backwards. The window’s largest size is the answer, found in one pass.' },
  21: { q: 'You may change up to k characters of the string into any other uppercase letter. What is the longest substring you can turn into a single repeated letter? For “AABABBA” with k = 1 the answer is 4 — change one letter in “ABBA”-like stretches to get “BBBB”.',
    a: 'Inside a window, the cheapest fix is to keep its most common letter and change everything else, which costs (window length − count of most common letter). The window is valid while that cost ≤ k. Expand to the right; when it becomes invalid, slide the left edge in by one. The answer only improves when a higher max count appears, so the max count never needs to be lowered — that keeps it O(n).' },
  22: { q: 'Does s2 contain some rearrangement of s1 as one contiguous block? For s1 = “ab” and s2 = “eidbaooo”, yes — “ba” appears. Only letter counts matter, and the block must be exactly s1’s length.',
    a: 'A permutation of s1 is any block with exactly s1’s letter counts. Every candidate block in s2 has the same length as s1, so slide a fixed-size window across s2: add the character coming in on the right, remove the one leaving on the left, and compare the window’s 26 counts with s1’s. Equal counts at any position means a match.' },
  23: { q: 'Slide a window of size k across the array from left to right, one step at a time, and report the largest number inside the window at every position. For n numbers there are n − k + 1 windows, so you return that many maximums.',
    a: 'Keep a deque of indices whose values are decreasing from front to back, so the front is always the current maximum. When a new number arrives, anything smaller in the back can never be a maximum again (the newcomer is bigger and stays longer), so pop it. Drop the front when it slides out of the window. Each index enters and leaves once, so it’s O(n) overall.' },
  24: { q: 'Find the shortest contiguous piece of s that contains every character of t, including repeats — if t is “AABC”, the piece needs two A’s. Return that piece, or an empty string if no such piece exists.',
    a: 'Expand a window to the right until it covers everything t needs, tracked by a counter of how many required characters are fully satisfied. Then shrink from the left as long as it stays valid, recording the window each time — that’s where the shortest answer appears. When shrinking breaks validity, expand again. Both edges only move forward, so it’s O(|s| + |t|).' },

  // ── Stack ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  26: { q: 'The string contains only brackets: ( ) [ ] { }. It is valid if every opening bracket is closed by the same type, in the correct order — the most recently opened bracket must close first. “([])” is valid; “([)]” isn’t, and neither is a lone “(”.',
    a: 'The most recently opened bracket must close first — last in, first out, exactly what a stack does. Push openers; for a closer, the top of the stack must be its matching opener, which you pop. A mismatch or an empty stack means invalid. At the end the stack must be empty, otherwise something was never closed.' },
  27: { q: 'Design a stack with the usual push, pop and top operations plus getMin, which returns the smallest value currently in the stack. Every operation, getMin included, must run in O(1) time.',
    a: 'The minimum only changes when you push or pop, so store it alongside each value: every entry remembers the minimum of the stack from the bottom up to itself. Push computes min(new value, previous top’s min). After a pop, the new top already knows the minimum of what’s left — no searching needed.' },
  28: { q: 'Evaluate an arithmetic expression written in Reverse Polish Notation, where each operator comes AFTER its two operands: [“2”, “1”, “+”, “3”, “*”] means (2 + 1) × 3 = 9. Division between integers truncates toward zero.',
    a: 'In RPN, every operator applies to the two most recent results — a stack keeps exactly those on top. Push numbers; on an operator, pop the right operand first, then the left, and push the result. Order matters for − and ÷. At the end, the single value left on the stack is the answer.' },
  29: { q: 'Given n, list every string of n pairs of parentheses that is correctly balanced. For n = 3 there are five: “((()))”, “(()())”, “(())()”, “()(())”, “()()()”.',
    a: 'Build strings one character at a time and only make choices that keep the string valid. You can add “(” while you haven’t used all n, and “)” only when it closes something still open (closed < opened). Because every prefix stays valid, every complete string of length 2n is a correct answer — no generating junk and filtering it later.' },
  30: { q: 'For each day’s temperature, find how many days you’d wait until a strictly warmer day. If no warmer day ever comes, the answer for that day is 0. For [73, 74, 75, 71, 69, 72, 76, 73] the answer is [1, 1, 4, 2, 1, 1, 0, 0].',
    a: 'Keep a stack of days still waiting for a warmer day; their temperatures are decreasing from bottom to top (otherwise they’d already have been answered). When a new day is warmer than the top, it’s the answer for that waiting day — pop it and record the distance, and repeat while it keeps beating the top. Then push today. Every day is pushed and popped once, so O(n).' },
  31: { q: 'Cars drive toward a target along a one-lane road, each with a start position and a speed. A faster car can’t pass a slower one ahead — it catches up and they move together as a “fleet” at the slower speed. How many fleets arrive at the target?',
    a: 'Look at cars from closest to the target to farthest. Compute when each would arrive if it drove alone. If a car would arrive later than the fleet ahead of it, it can never catch up — it starts a new fleet. If it would arrive at the same time or sooner, it gets blocked and merges into that fleet. Sorting by position plus a stack of arrival times counts the fleets.' },
  32: { q: 'The array gives the heights of side-by-side bars of width 1. Find the area of the largest rectangle that fits completely inside the histogram — its height is limited by the shortest bar it spans.',
    a: 'Each bar’s best rectangle uses that bar as the height and stretches left and right until a shorter bar blocks it. A stack of bars with increasing heights finds those limits: when a shorter bar arrives, every taller bar on the stack has found its right edge, and the bar beneath it in the stack is its left edge. Pop, compute the area, continue. Every bar is pushed and popped once — O(n).' },

  // ── Binary Search ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  34: { q: 'Search a sorted array of distinct numbers for a target value and return its index, or −1 if it isn’t there. The required time is O(log n), so scanning every element isn’t good enough.',
    a: 'Compare the target with the middle element. If the middle is too small, the target can only be in the right half; too big, only in the left half. Each comparison throws away half of what’s left, so you need at most about log₂ n steps. Compute mid as left + (right − left) / 2 to avoid integer overflow.' },
  35: { q: 'Each row of the matrix is sorted, and every row starts with a value bigger than the last value of the row above. Decide whether a target value is in the matrix, in O(log(m·n)) time.',
    a: 'Because each row continues where the previous one ended, reading the rows one after another gives a single sorted list of m·n numbers. Binary search that imaginary list by index, converting index i to the cell matrix[i / cols][i % cols]. No need to flatten anything.' },
  36: { q: 'Koko has piles of bananas and h hours. Each hour she picks one pile and eats up to k bananas from it (if the pile has fewer, she finishes it and waits for the next hour). Find the smallest eating speed k that lets her finish every pile within h hours.',
    a: 'You can’t compute k directly, but you can test a guess: at speed k she needs the sum of ceil(pile / k) hours. Faster speeds never need more hours, so the answers look like “too slow, too slow, …, fast enough, fast enough”. Binary search over speeds 1 to max(pile) finds the first “fast enough”. This is “binary search on the answer”.' },
  37: { q: 'A sorted array of distinct numbers was rotated: some prefix was cut off and moved to the end, e.g. [0,1,2,4,5,6,7] became [4,5,6,7,0,1,2]. Find the smallest element in O(log n).',
    a: 'The minimum sits right where the order “drops”. Compare the middle element with the rightmost one. If the middle is bigger, the drop must be to its right, so the minimum is right of mid. Otherwise mid..right is sorted and the minimum is at mid or to its left. Halve the range until one element remains.' },
  38: { q: 'A sorted array of distinct numbers has been rotated at some unknown point. Find the index of a target value, or −1 if it’s missing, in O(log n) time.',
    a: 'Split at the middle: at least one half is always properly sorted, and you can tell which by comparing its ends. For the sorted half, a simple range check tells you whether the target can be inside it. If it can, search there; if not, it must be in the other half. Each step still halves the search, so it stays O(log n).' },
  39: { q: 'Design a key-value store that keeps every version of a value. set(key, value, timestamp) saves a version; get(key, timestamp) returns the value set at the latest time that is ≤ timestamp, or "" if none. Timestamps for set always increase.',
    a: 'Store each key’s versions in a list. Since set is always called with increasing timestamps, each list is already sorted by time. A get is then “find the last version with time ≤ timestamp”, which binary search answers in O(log n).' },
  40: { q: 'Find the median of two sorted arrays as if they were merged into one, in O(log(m + n)) time. With an odd total the median is the middle value; with an even total it’s the average of the two middle values.',
    a: 'The median splits the merged array into a left half and a right half. Choose how many elements the left half takes from the shorter array (i); the rest come from the other (j = half − i). The split is correct when every left element ≤ every right element — just compare the four values around the cut. Binary search on i fixes it in O(log(min(m, n))).' },

  // ── Linked List ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  42: { q: 'Reverse a singly linked list: make every node point to the one that used to come before it, and return the new head (the old tail). 1→2→3 becomes 3→2→1. An empty list stays empty.',
    a: 'Walk the list once and flip each arrow as you pass it. You need three pointers: prev (already reversed), curr (the one you’re flipping) and next (saved before flipping, or you’d lose the rest of the list). When curr runs off the end, prev is the new head.' },
  43: { q: 'Merge two sorted linked lists into one sorted list by relinking their existing nodes, and return its head. Either list may be empty.',
    a: 'The smallest remaining node is always at the head of one of the two lists, so repeatedly take the smaller head and attach it to your result. A dummy starting node means there’s no special case for picking the first node. When one list runs out, attach the rest of the other — it’s already sorted.' },
  44: { q: 'Decide whether a linked list has a cycle — some node’s next pointer leads back to an earlier node, so following next forever never reaches null.',
    a: 'Send two runners down the list, one moving 1 step at a time and the other 2. If there’s an end, the fast one reaches it. If there’s a loop, both end up circling it, and the fast runner gains one node per step on the slow one — so it must land on it eventually. Meeting means a cycle, with O(1) memory.' },
  45: { q: 'Reorder the list L0→L1→…→Ln into L0→Ln→L1→Ln−1→L2→… by relinking nodes in place — the first, then the last, then the second, then the second-to-last, and so on.',
    a: 'The new order alternates between the front half (forwards) and the back half (backwards). So: find the middle with slow/fast pointers, cut the list there, reverse the second half, then weave the two halves together one node at a time. Three standard linked-list moves, O(n) time and O(1) space.' },
  46: { q: 'Remove the nth node counted from the END of the list and return the head. With 1→2→3→4→5 and n = 2, remove 4. Removing the head itself must work too.',
    a: 'Put two pointers n + 1 nodes apart, then move them together. When the front pointer falls off the end, the back pointer is exactly one node before the one to delete, so you can skip over it. A dummy node in front of the head covers the case where the head itself is removed. One pass.' },
  47: { q: 'Each node has a next pointer and a random pointer that can point to any node or to null. Build a completely separate deep copy, where the copy’s next and random pointers point to copied nodes — never back into the original list.',
    a: 'The difficulty is that a random pointer may lead to a node you haven’t copied yet. Solve it in two passes with a map from original node to its copy: first create every copy, then wire each copy’s next and random by looking up the copies of the originals’ targets. Since every copy already exists, every lookup succeeds.' },
  48: { q: 'Two non-negative numbers are stored as linked lists of digits in REVERSE order (the ones digit first). Add them and return the sum as a list in the same reversed format. The lists can have different lengths.',
    a: 'Reverse order is a gift: the ones digits come first, which is exactly how you add by hand. Walk both lists together, adding the two digits plus the carry; the new digit is the sum % 10 and the carry is the sum / 10. Treat a finished list as 0, and keep going while there’s a carry left.' },
  49: { q: 'The array has n + 1 numbers, each between 1 and n, so at least one value repeats — and exactly one value does, possibly several times. Find it without changing the array and using only O(1) extra space.',
    a: 'Treat each index as a node with an arrow to nums[i]. Because two indices hold the duplicate value, two arrows point at the same node — so following arrows from index 0 enters a cycle, and the duplicate is where the cycle begins. Floyd’s algorithm finds that entrance: fast and slow pointers meet inside the loop, then a fresh pointer from the start and the slow one meet exactly at the entrance.' },
  50: { q: 'Design a cache with a fixed capacity. get(key) returns the value or −1; put(key, value) inserts or updates. When it’s full, putting a new key evicts the Least Recently Used key — the one untouched for the longest. Both operations must be O(1).',
    a: 'You need two things in O(1): find a key, and know which key is the oldest. A hash map finds nodes; a doubly linked list keeps them in usage order. Every get or put moves the node to the front, so the least recently used is always at the back, ready to evict. Doubly linked means removing a node from the middle is O(1).' },
  51: { q: 'Merge k sorted linked lists into one sorted linked list and return it. Some lists may be empty, and k can be large, so merging them one by one into a growing result is too slow.',
    a: 'At any moment, the next node of the merged list is the smallest among the k current heads. A min-heap holds those k heads and hands you the smallest in O(log k). Take it, then push that node’s successor. Every one of the N nodes goes through the heap once, so the total is O(N log k).' },
  52: { q: 'Reverse the nodes of the list k at a time: 1→2→3→4→5 with k = 2 becomes 2→1→4→3→5. A final group with fewer than k nodes stays as it is. Only change links, not node values.',
    a: 'Handle one group at a time. First check that k nodes exist; if not, stop. Reverse those k nodes with the usual three-pointer reversal, but start prev at the node AFTER the group — so the group’s old first node automatically links to what follows. Then connect the node before the group to the new group head, and move on.' },

  // ── Trees ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  55: { q: 'Mirror a binary tree: swap the left and right child of every node, all the way down, and return the root. The tree you’d see in a mirror is the answer.',
    a: 'Mirroring a tree means mirroring both subtrees and then swapping them. Recursion does exactly that: invert the left subtree, invert the right subtree, swap them. An empty tree is already mirrored. Every node is visited once.' },
  56: { q: 'Return the maximum depth of a binary tree — the number of nodes on the longest path from the root down to a leaf. An empty tree has depth 0, a lone root has depth 1.',
    a: 'A tree’s depth is one (for the root) plus the depth of its deeper subtree. Ask each child for its depth recursively and combine the answers on the way back up. An empty subtree reports 0, which ends the recursion.' },
  57: { q: 'The diameter is the length (in edges) of the longest path between ANY two nodes in the tree. That path doesn’t have to pass through the root — it can bend inside a subtree.',
    a: 'Every path has a highest point where it turns from going up to going down. The longest path turning at a node is (height of left subtree) + (height of right subtree). So one height-computing DFS can, at each node, also check that sum against a global best — the answer may be found deep in the tree, not at the root.' },
  58: { q: 'A binary tree is height-balanced if, at EVERY node, the heights of the left and right subtrees differ by at most 1. Decide whether the given tree is balanced.',
    a: 'Calling a height function at every node recomputes heights again and again (O(n²)). Instead, compute heights bottom-up once and check balance on the way: each node gets its children’s heights, and if they differ by more than 1 (or a child is already unbalanced) it reports −1, which travels up to the root. One pass, O(n).' },
  59: { q: 'Two binary trees are the same if they have exactly the same shape and the same value at every matching node. Decide whether the two given trees are the same.',
    a: 'Walk both trees in lockstep. Two empty nodes match; one empty and one not, or different values, don’t. Otherwise the trees are the same exactly when both left subtrees are the same AND both right subtrees are the same. Checking the base cases first keeps it safe.' },
  60: { q: 'Decide whether subRoot appears inside root as a complete subtree: some node of root, together with ALL of its descendants, must look exactly like subRoot. Matching only part of a subtree doesn’t count.',
    a: 'A matching subtree has to start at some node of root. So try every node as a starting point and run the Same Tree check from there. If any node matches, the answer is true. That’s O(m·n) in the worst case, which is fine for this problem.' },
  61: { q: 'In a binary SEARCH tree, find the lowest common ancestor of nodes p and q — the deepest node that has both p and q in its subtree. A node counts as an ancestor of itself.',
    a: 'In a BST, smaller values go left and larger go right. From the root, if p and q are both smaller, their common ancestor must be in the left subtree; if both larger, in the right. The first node where they split (one on each side, or one equals the node) is the lowest common ancestor. No need to search the whole tree.' },
  62: { q: 'Return the node values level by level: a list for the root’s level, then a list for the next level, and so on, each read left to right. [3,9,20,null,null,15,7] gives [[3],[9,20],[15,7]].',
    a: 'A queue visits nodes in the order they were discovered, which naturally goes level by level. The trick for separating levels: at the start of each round, the queue holds exactly one level. Take its size, pop that many nodes into the current list, and push their children for the next round.' },
  63: { q: 'Imagine standing to the right of the tree. Return the values you can see from top to bottom — the rightmost node of each level. A left node can be visible if nothing is to its right on that level.',
    a: 'The visible node on each level is simply the last one on that level from left to right. Do a level-order traversal (BFS) and record the last node of each level. Equivalently, a DFS that visits right before left records the first node it sees at each new depth.' },
  64: { q: 'Decide whether a binary tree is a valid binary search tree: every node’s left subtree must hold only values smaller than it, and its right subtree only values larger — for the WHOLE subtree, not just the direct children.',
    a: 'Checking a node against its direct children isn’t enough; a node deep in the left subtree must still be smaller than an ancestor far above. So pass down an allowed range (low, high). Going left, the current value becomes the new upper bound; going right, the new lower bound. Any value outside its range breaks the BST.' },
  65: { q: 'Return the kth smallest value in a binary search tree, counting from 1. In a BST with values 1 to 4, k = 1 gives 1 and k = 3 gives 3.',
    a: 'An in-order traversal (left, node, right) of a BST visits values in increasing order. So walk it and count: the kth node you visit is the answer. Doing it iteratively with a stack lets you stop the moment you reach k instead of visiting the whole tree.' },
  66: { q: 'You get the preorder and inorder traversals of a binary tree with distinct values. Rebuild the original tree and return its root.',
    a: 'Preorder always lists a subtree’s root first. Find that root in the inorder list: everything to its left is the left subtree, everything to its right is the right subtree. Recurse on both sides, taking roots from preorder in order — left subtree first, since preorder lists it first. A value → inorder-index map makes each split O(1).' },
  67: { q: 'A path is any chain of connected nodes, used at most once each, that can start and end anywhere — it doesn’t have to go through the root. Return the largest possible sum of values along a path. Values can be negative, and the path must have at least one node.',
    a: 'Every path has a top node where it can use both branches. Compute, for each node, the best downward “gain” it can offer its parent — its value plus its better child branch, ignoring negative branches (count them as 0). At each node also consider the bent path: value + left gain + right gain, and update a global best. One post-order pass.' },

  // ── Tries ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  69: { q: 'Build a prefix tree (trie) supporting insert(word), search(word) — was exactly this word inserted? — and startsWith(prefix) — was any inserted word starting with this prefix?',
    a: 'A trie stores words letter by letter along a path from the root, so words sharing a prefix share the same path. Insert walks the path, creating missing nodes, and flags the last node as a word end. search and startsWith both walk the path; the only difference is that search also requires that end flag.' },
  70: { q: 'Design a word dictionary with addWord(word) and search(word), where search may contain “.” characters that match any single letter. Searching “b..” finds “bad” or “bed” if either was added.',
    a: 'Store words in a trie. Normal letters follow one child, just like a regular lookup. A “.” can be any letter, so try every child of the current node and succeed if any branch finds the rest of the word. Only the wildcards cause branching.' },
  71: { q: 'Given a grid of letters and a list of words, return every word that can be traced in the grid by moving between horizontally or vertically adjacent cells, using each cell at most once per word.',
    a: 'Searching for each word separately repeats a lot of work. Put all the words into a trie and run one DFS from each cell that follows the trie as it walks the grid — a path that isn’t a prefix of any word stops right away. Whenever the DFS reaches a node marking a complete word, record it (and clear it so it’s not found twice).' },

  // ── Heap / Priority Queue ───────────────────────────────────────
  73: { q: 'Design a class that keeps receiving numbers from a stream. After each add(val), return the kth largest number seen so far (the kth in sorted order, not the kth distinct).',
    a: 'You only ever care about the k largest numbers. Keep them in a min-heap of size k: its smallest element — the top — is exactly the kth largest. When a new number arrives, push it, and if the heap grows past k, pop the smallest. Each add is O(log k).' },
  74: { q: 'Each turn, smash the two heaviest stones together. Equal weights destroy both; otherwise the lighter is destroyed and the heavier loses that much weight. Repeat until at most one stone is left, and return its weight (or 0).',
    a: 'Each turn needs the two largest stones, and the leftover goes back in — a max-heap gives you the largest in O(log n) and accepts the new stone just as fast. Simulate until fewer than two stones remain.' },
  75: { q: 'Given points on a plane, return the k points closest to the origin (0, 0) by straight-line distance. The answer is unique, and any order is fine.',
    a: 'Comparing squared distances (x² + y²) gives the same order as real distances without any square roots. Keep a max-heap of the k closest points so far: the farthest of them sits on top, so whenever the heap grows past k you pop the worst candidate. What’s left are the k closest. O(n log k).' },
  76: { q: 'Return the kth largest element of an unsorted array — the element that would be at position k if the array were sorted in decreasing order. Duplicates count separately.',
    a: 'You don’t need the whole array sorted, only the top k. A min-heap of size k keeps the k largest values seen; after scanning everything, its smallest (the top) is the kth largest. Quickselect, which partitions like quicksort but only recurses into one side, gets O(n) on average if you’re asked for faster.' },
  77: { q: 'Tasks are letters; each takes one time unit. Two runs of the SAME task must be at least n units apart, and the CPU may idle. Tasks can run in any order. What’s the minimum total time to run them all?',
    a: 'The most frequent task decides the shape: its copies must be n + 1 apart, creating (maxFreq − 1) blocks of length n + 1 followed by a final row containing every task that shares the top frequency. Other tasks fill the gaps in those blocks. If there are more tasks than gaps, no idling is needed at all and the answer is just the number of tasks.' },
  78: { q: 'Design a mini Twitter: users post tweets, follow and unfollow each other, and getNewsFeed(user) returns the 10 most recent tweet ids from the user and everyone they follow, newest first.',
    a: 'Each user’s own tweets are already in time order. The feed is the newest 10 across several such lists — exactly “merge k sorted lists”, stopped after 10. Put each relevant user’s newest tweet into a max-heap by time; pop the newest, then push the next-older tweet from the same user. A global counter gives every tweet a time.' },
  79: { q: 'Design a structure that receives numbers one at a time (addNum) and can report the median of everything so far (findMedian) at any moment. Re-sorting on every call is too slow.',
    a: 'Split the numbers into a lower half and an upper half. Keep the lower half in a max-heap and the upper half in a min-heap, so their tops are the two numbers around the middle. After each insert, rebalance so every lower value ≤ every upper value and the sizes differ by at most one. The median is then read off the tops in O(1).' },

  // ── Backtracking ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  81: { q: 'Return every subset (the power set) of an array of distinct numbers, including the empty set and the array itself. n numbers give 2ⁿ subsets; no duplicates, any order.',
    a: 'Each number is either in or out of a subset. Backtracking builds subsets in increasing index order: from position start, choose a number, recurse to pick more numbers after it, then un-choose it. Every point in this recursion is a valid subset, so record it every time. Moving only forward guarantees each subset is built once.' },
  82: { q: 'Find every combination of the candidate numbers (all distinct) that adds up to the target. A number can be used any number of times. Combinations are sets: [2,2,3] and [2,3,2] are the same one.',
    a: 'Build combinations in non-decreasing order to avoid duplicates: from a candidate index, either use the current number again (stay at the same index) or move on to later ones. Subtract as you go; reaching exactly 0 is a solution. With sorted candidates, you can stop as soon as a candidate is bigger than what remains.' },
  83: { q: 'Return every ordering (permutation) of an array of distinct numbers. n numbers have n! permutations, e.g. [1,2,3] has 6.',
    a: 'A permutation fills positions one at a time, and each position may take any number not used yet. Backtracking does exactly that: mark a number used, append it, recurse to fill the next position, then undo both. When all n are placed, record a copy. Unlike combinations, every unused number is tried at each step — order matters here.' },
  84: { q: 'Return every distinct subset of an array that may contain duplicates. With [1,2,2], the subset [1,2] should appear once even though there are two 2s to choose from.',
    a: 'Sort so equal numbers sit together. Then use the Subsets backtracking, with one extra rule: at the same level of recursion, never start a branch with a value equal to the previous one (skip nums[i] when i > start and nums[i] == nums[i − 1]). Deeper levels may still use the second copy, so [2,2] is allowed — only duplicate branches are cut.' },
  85: { q: 'Decide whether a word can be traced in a grid of letters by moving between horizontally or vertically adjacent cells, using each cell at most once.',
    a: 'Try starting from every cell. From a cell matching the next letter, explore its four neighbours for the following letter, depth-first. Mark the cell as used while you’re inside it, and restore it when you back out so other paths can use it. Reaching the end of the word means it exists.' },
  86: { q: 'Split the string into pieces where every piece is a palindrome, and return every possible way to do it. “aab” gives [“a”,“a”,“b”] and [“aa”,“b”].',
    a: 'Choose the first piece, then solve the rest of the string the same way. From the current position, try every end point; if that prefix is a palindrome, keep it and recurse on what remains. When you reach the end of the string, the pieces you’ve chosen form one valid split.' },
  87: { q: 'Place n queens on an n×n chessboard so that no two attack each other — no shared row, column or diagonal. Return every valid board, drawn with “Q” for queens and “.” for empty squares.',
    a: 'Every row has exactly one queen, so place them row by row and only choose the column. A square is attacked if its column, its diagonal (row − col) or its anti-diagonal (row + col) is already taken — three sets make that check O(1). Place, recurse to the next row, then remove the queen and try the next column.' },

  // ── Graphs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  89: { q: 'The grid holds “1” (land) and “0” (water). An island is a group of land cells connected up, down, left or right. Count the islands.',
    a: 'Scan the grid. The first land cell of an unseen island adds one to the count, and then a flood fill (DFS or BFS) sinks its whole island so it’s never counted again. Every cell is visited a constant number of times, so it’s O(rows × cols).' },
  90: { q: 'Make a deep copy of a connected undirected graph given one node. Each node has a value and a list of neighbours; the copy must be made of brand-new nodes wired exactly the same way.',
    a: 'Walk the graph with DFS while keeping a map from each original node to its copy. When you reach a node that’s already in the map, return its copy instead of making another — that’s what stops cycles from looping forever. Put the copy in the map BEFORE cloning its neighbours.' },
  91: { q: 'The grid has 1 for land and 0 for water. An island is land connected up, down, left or right; its area is its number of cells. Return the largest island’s area, or 0 if there’s no land.',
    a: 'It’s Number of Islands, except the flood fill also counts. A DFS from a land cell sinks it and returns 1 plus the areas returned by its four neighbours. Run it from every cell and keep the biggest result.' },
  92: { q: 'Rain flows from a cell to a neighbour with equal or lower height. The Pacific touches the top and left edges, the Atlantic the bottom and right. Return every cell from which water can reach BOTH oceans.',
    a: 'Checking every cell’s downhill paths separately is wasteful. Turn it around: start at each ocean’s border cells and climb UPHILL (to neighbours with equal or greater height). Every cell you can climb to can drain into that ocean. Do it for both oceans and return the cells reached by both.' },
  93: { q: 'The board has “X” and “O” cells. Capture every region of “O”s that is completely surrounded by “X” by turning it into “X”. A region touching the border can’t be surrounded, so it stays.',
    a: 'It’s easier to find the regions that survive. Any “O” connected to the border survives, so flood-fill from every border “O” and mark those cells safe. Every “O” that wasn’t marked is surrounded — flip it to “X”, then turn the safe marks back into “O”.' },
  94: { q: 'In the grid, 0 is empty, 1 a fresh orange and 2 a rotten one. Every minute, rotten oranges rot their fresh neighbours (up, down, left, right). Return the minutes until no fresh orange is left, or −1 if some can never rot.',
    a: 'Rot spreads outward one step per minute from every rotten orange at the same time — that’s BFS with many starting points. Put ALL rotten oranges in the queue at minute 0 and process the queue one level per minute. If fresh oranges remain when the queue is empty, they were unreachable.' },
  95: { q: 'For every cell of a 0/1 matrix, return the distance to the nearest 0, moving up, down, left or right. Cells that are 0 have distance 0.',
    a: 'Instead of searching from every 1, search from all the 0s at once. Put every 0 in the queue with distance 0 and run BFS: each newly reached cell gets its parent’s distance + 1. BFS reaches each cell first from its closest 0, so the first distance written is correct. O(rows × cols).' },
  96: { q: 'There are numCourses courses, and a pair [a, b] means you must take b before a. Can you finish every course? It’s impossible exactly when the prerequisites contain a cycle.',
    a: 'Model courses as a directed graph and count each course’s unmet prerequisites (indegree). Courses with none can be taken; taking one lowers its dependents’ counts, freeing more courses. That’s Kahn’s topological sort. If some courses never reach zero, they’re stuck in a cycle.' },
  97: { q: 'Same setup as Course Schedule, but return an order in which to take every course that respects all prerequisites. Any valid order works; return an empty list if no order exists.',
    a: 'Kahn’s algorithm already takes courses in a valid order — a course only enters the queue once everything it depends on has been taken. So record courses as they come off the queue. If the list ends shorter than numCourses, a cycle blocked the rest, and the answer is empty.' },
  98: { q: 'A tree with n nodes had one extra edge added, creating exactly one cycle. Given the edges, return an edge whose removal leaves a tree again. If several qualify, return the one that appears last in the input.',
    a: 'Add edges one by one while tracking connected groups with Union-Find. An edge whose two ends are already in the same group connects things that were already connected — it closes the cycle, so it’s the extra one. Union-Find makes each check nearly O(1).' },
  99: { q: 'isConnected[i][j] = 1 means cities i and j are directly connected. A province is a group of cities connected directly or through other cities. Count the provinces.',
    a: 'Provinces are just connected components. Loop over the cities; each one not yet visited starts a new province, and a DFS from it marks every city reachable from it so they aren’t counted again. The number of DFS starts is the answer.' },
  100: { q: 'n computers are linked by cables. You may unplug any cable and reconnect it between two other computers. What’s the minimum number of moves to connect every computer, or −1 if it’s impossible?',
    a: 'Connecting n computers needs at least n − 1 cables; with fewer it’s impossible. With enough cables there’s always a spare one inside some group (a redundant cable), so each move can join two separate groups. With c separate groups you need exactly c − 1 moves. Count groups with Union-Find.' },
  101: { q: 'Turn beginWord into endWord by changing one letter at a time, where every intermediate word must be in the word list. Return the number of words in the shortest such sequence (counting both ends), or 0 if it can’t be done.',
    a: 'Words are nodes, and two words are connected if they differ in one letter — the shortest sequence is a shortest path, which BFS finds. To find neighbours quickly, group words by patterns with one letter blanked out (“h*t”): words sharing a pattern are one step apart. BFS level by level counts the steps.' },

  // ── Advanced Graphs ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  103: { q: 'A network has n nodes and directed edges with travel times. A signal is sent from node k. How long until EVERY node has received it? Return −1 if some node never does.',
    a: 'Each node receives the signal at its shortest travel time from k, and everyone has it when the slowest of those arrives. Shortest paths with non-negative weights is Dijkstra: always settle the closest unsettled node next, using a min-heap. The answer is the largest settled time, or −1 if some node was never reached.' },
  104: { q: 'Given flights with prices, find the cheapest way from src to dst using at most k stops (so at most k + 1 flights). Return −1 if no such route exists.',
    a: 'The limit is on the number of flights, so build answers flight by flight. Bellman-Ford round r finds the cheapest price to every city using at most r flights. Run k + 1 rounds, and in each round update from a COPY of the previous round’s prices — otherwise one round could chain several flights and break the stop limit.' },
  105: { q: 'Connect all the points on a plane so every point is reachable from every other, where joining two points costs their Manhattan distance |x1 − x2| + |y1 − y2|. Return the minimum total cost.',
    a: 'This is a minimum spanning tree on a graph where every pair of points is an edge. Prim’s algorithm grows the tree one point at a time, always adding the point that’s cheapest to attach. Since the graph is complete (about n² edges), a simple array of “cheapest connection to the tree” gives O(n²) without a heap.' },
  106: { q: 'Given airline tickets [from, to], rebuild the itinerary starting at “JFK” that uses every ticket exactly once. If several itineraries work, return the one that’s smallest in alphabetical order.',
    a: 'Using every ticket once is an Eulerian path. Hierholzer’s algorithm: from an airport, keep taking its smallest remaining ticket and continue from there. When you’re stuck at an airport with no tickets left, add it to the route. Building the route at those dead ends (in post-order) and reversing it gives a path that uses every ticket, in alphabetical order.' },
  107: { q: 'Each cell has an elevation, and at time t the water level is t. You can swim between adjacent cells only when both are at most t. Starting at the top-left, what’s the earliest time you can reach the bottom-right?',
    a: 'A route is usable once the water reaches its HIGHEST cell, so you want the path whose highest point is as low as possible. Dijkstra works with “cost = highest cell so far” instead of a sum: always expand the cell reachable with the lowest such maximum. The first time you pop the bottom-right corner, that maximum is the answer.' },
  108: { q: 'Each item may belong to a group (or −1 for none) and has a list of items that must come before it. Order the items so every “before” rule holds AND items of the same group stay next to each other. Return [] if impossible.',
    a: 'Two kinds of ordering are needed: groups among themselves, and items inside each group. Give every ungrouped item its own group, then build two graphs — items with item edges, and groups with an edge whenever a rule crosses groups. Topologically sort both; a cycle in either means it’s impossible. Finally list the groups in order, each with its items in item order.' },

  // ── 1-D DP ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  110: { q: 'You climb a staircase of n steps, taking 1 or 2 steps at a time. In how many different ways can you reach the top? For n = 3 there are 3 ways: 1+1+1, 1+2 and 2+1.',
    a: 'Consider the last move to step n: it came either from step n − 1 (a 1-step) or from step n − 2 (a 2-step). So ways(n) = ways(n − 1) + ways(n − 2) — the Fibonacci numbers. You only need the previous two values, so two variables are enough.' },
  111: { q: 'cost[i] is what you pay to step off stair i, after which you can climb 1 or 2 stairs. You may start on stair 0 or stair 1 for free. Reach the top, one past the last stair, at minimum total cost.',
    a: 'To stand on stair i you must have come from i − 1 or i − 2, paying the cost of the stair you left. So the cheapest way to reach i is the cheaper of those two options. Build this up from the free starts (stairs 0 and 1 cost 0 to stand on) to the top; two rolling variables are enough.' },
  112: { q: 'Houses along a street hold money. You can’t rob two ADJACENT houses (the alarm goes off). What’s the most money you can take?',
    a: 'At each house you either skip it, keeping the best total up to the previous house, or rob it, adding its money to the best total up to two houses back (since the neighbour can’t be robbed). Take the larger. Only the last two totals matter, so two variables are enough.' },
  113: { q: 'Same as House Robber, but the houses are in a circle, so the first and the last house are also neighbours and can’t both be robbed.',
    a: 'The circle only adds one conflict: first versus last. Any valid plan skips at least one of them. So solve the straight-line House Robber twice — once without the last house, once without the first — and take the better result. A single house is a special case.' },
  114: { q: 'Return the longest contiguous substring that reads the same forwards and backwards. In “babad” both “bab” and “aba” are correct answers.',
    a: 'Every palindrome mirrors around a centre — either one character (odd length) or the gap between two (even length). There are 2n − 1 centres. From each, expand outward while the two ends match; that finds the longest palindrome around that centre. Keep the longest overall. O(n²) time, O(1) space.' },
  115: { q: 'Count how many substrings of the string are palindromes. Substrings at different positions count separately, even if they read the same — “aaa” has 6: “a”, “a”, “a”, “aa”, “aa”, “aaa”.',
    a: 'Use the same centre expansion as Longest Palindromic Substring. Every successful step outward from a centre reveals one more palindrome, so add one each time the two ends match. Summing over all 2n − 1 centres counts every palindromic substring exactly once.' },
  116: { q: 'Letters are encoded as numbers: A = 1, B = 2, …, Z = 26. Given a string of digits, count how many ways it can be decoded. “226” has 3 ways: “2 2 6”, “22 6”, “2 26”. A “0” can’t stand alone, and “06” isn’t valid.',
    a: 'Look at the end of the string: the last letter used either one digit or two. One digit works if it isn’t “0” — that adds the number of ways to decode everything before it. Two digits work if they form 10–26 — that adds the ways for everything before those two. It’s Climbing Stairs with conditions, needing only two rolling values.' },
  117: { q: 'Given coin values (unlimited coins of each) and an amount, find the fewest coins that add up exactly to that amount, or −1 if it can’t be made.',
    a: 'Always taking the biggest coin can fail (coins [1,3,4] for 6: greedy uses 4+1+1, but 3+3 is better). Instead, find the best answer for every amount from 0 up: the best way to make a ends with some coin c, so it’s 1 + best(a − c), minimised over all coins. Smaller amounts are already solved when you need them.' },
  118: { q: 'Find the contiguous subarray with the largest product and return that product. Numbers can be negative or zero — e.g. [2,3,−2,4] gives 6, from [2,3].',
    a: 'A negative number flips things: the most negative product so far can become the largest after multiplying by another negative. So for each position, track both the maximum and the minimum product of a subarray ending there. The new values come from the number alone, the number × old max, or the number × old min.' },
  119: { q: 'Can the string be split into a sequence of dictionary words, with no characters left over? Words may be reused. “applepenapple” with [“apple”, “pen”] can.',
    a: 'Let dp[i] mean “the first i characters can be split”. The empty prefix can (dp[0] = true). dp[i] is true when some earlier dp[j] is true and the piece from j to i is a dictionary word. Fill dp from left to right; dp[n] is the answer.' },
  120: { q: 'Find the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence. A subsequence keeps the original order but can skip elements: in [10,9,2,5,3,7,101,18] one longest is [2,3,7,101], so the answer is 4.',
    a: 'Keep tails[k] = the smallest possible last value of an increasing subsequence of length k + 1. A smaller tail is always better, because more numbers can follow it. For each number, binary search the first tail ≥ it: replace that tail, or extend the list if the number is bigger than every tail. The list’s final length is the answer. O(n log n).' },
  121: { q: 'Can the array be split into two groups with equal sums? Every number goes into exactly one group.',
    a: 'Two equal halves means one group sums to total / 2 (an odd total is impossible). So the question becomes: is there a subset adding up to target? Track which sums are reachable with a boolean array, adding numbers one at a time. Looping the sums downwards ensures each number is used at most once — 0/1 knapsack.' },

  // ── 2-D DP ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  123: { q: 'A robot starts at the top-left of an m × n grid and can only move right or down. How many different paths lead to the bottom-right corner?',
    a: 'Every cell is reached either from the cell above or from the cell to the left, so its path count is the sum of those two. The first row and first column have exactly one path each. Filling the grid row by row needs only one row of memory.' },
  124: { q: 'Find the length of the longest sequence of characters that appears in both strings in the same order, not necessarily contiguous. “abcde” and “ace” share “ace”, length 3.',
    a: 'Compare the last characters of two prefixes. If they’re equal, they can end the common subsequence: 1 + the answer without both. If not, at least one of them isn’t used, so take the better of dropping one character from either string. A table over all prefix pairs fills this in O(m·n).' },
  125: { q: 'Given daily prices, make as many buy/sell trades as you like, holding at most one share at a time — but after selling you must wait one day (cooldown) before buying again. Find the maximum profit.',
    a: 'Each day you’re in one of three situations: holding a share, just sold (so tomorrow is a cooldown), or resting with no share and free to buy. Write how each state follows from yesterday’s: hold = keep holding or buy from rest; sold = sell what you held; rest = stay resting or finish the cooldown. The best final state without a share is the answer.' },
  126: { q: 'Count the number of different combinations of coins (unlimited of each) that make up the amount. Order doesn’t matter: 1+2 and 2+1 are the same combination.',
    a: 'To count combinations rather than orderings, decide coin by coin. With the coin loop OUTSIDE, each coin type is added in a fixed order, so every combination is counted once. dp[a] += dp[a − coin] adds the ways that use this coin at least once, and looping a upward lets the same coin be reused.' },
  127: { q: 'Put a “+” or “−” in front of every number, and count how many sign choices make the total exactly equal to the target.',
    a: 'Split the numbers into a plus group P and a minus group N. Then P − N = target and P + N = total, so P = (total + target) / 2. The question becomes “how many subsets sum to P?” — a counting 0/1 knapsack. If total + target is odd or |target| > total, there are 0 ways.' },
  128: { q: 'Decide whether s3 can be formed by interleaving s1 and s2 — using all characters of both, keeping each string’s own character order, mixing them in any pattern.',
    a: 'Greedily picking from whichever string matches can fail when both match. Let dp[i][j] mean “the first i of s1 and the first j of s2 can form the first i + j of s3”. It’s true if the last character came from s1 and it matches (with dp[i − 1][j] true) or came from s2 and matches (with dp[i][j − 1] true).' },
  129: { q: 'Find the minimum number of single-character operations — insert, delete or replace — needed to turn word1 into word2.',
    a: 'Look at the last characters of two prefixes. If they’re equal, no edit is needed for them. Otherwise one of three edits fixes the end: replace (both prefixes shrink), delete from word1 (word1 shrinks) or insert into word1 (word2 shrinks) — pay 1 and take the cheapest. Turning a prefix into an empty string costs its length.' },
  130: { q: 'From any cell of the matrix you may move up, down, left or right to a strictly LARGER neighbour. Return the length of the longest path (counted in cells) you can make.',
    a: 'From a cell, the longest path is 1 plus the longest path from any larger neighbour — and that neighbour’s answer never depends on where you came from. Because values must strictly increase, a path can never loop back, so no visited set is needed. Memoise each cell’s answer and every cell is solved once.' },
  131: { q: 'Count how many different ways you can delete characters from s (keeping the order) so that what remains is exactly t. Ways are different if they keep different positions of s.',
    a: 'Compare s[i] with t[j]. You can always skip s[i] and match t from the rest of s. If the characters are equal, you can also use s[i] to match t[j] and move both forward. The count is the sum of those options. Once t is fully matched there’s exactly one way. Fill the table from the back.' },

  // ── Greedy ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  133: { q: 'Find the contiguous subarray (at least one number) with the largest sum and return that sum. For [−2,1,−3,4,−1,2,1,−5,4] it’s 6, from [4,−1,2,1].',
    a: 'Scan left to right with the best sum of a subarray ending at the current number. If that running sum has gone negative, it can only drag down whatever comes next, so start again from the current number. Track the best value seen. That’s Kadane’s algorithm — one pass, and it handles all-negative arrays by starting from the first number.' },
  134: { q: 'You start at index 0, and nums[i] is the MAXIMUM jump length from position i. Can you reach the last index?',
    a: 'Keep track of the farthest index you can reach so far. Walk left to right: if the current index lies beyond that reach, you can never get here, so the answer is false. Otherwise extend the reach with i + nums[i]. If you get through the array, the end is reachable.' },
  135: { q: 'Same jumping rules as Jump Game, and reaching the end is guaranteed. Return the minimum number of jumps needed to reach the last index.',
    a: 'Think in rounds like BFS: everything reachable with j jumps forms a range ending at end. While scanning that range, record the farthest you could reach with one more jump. When you hit the end of the current range, you must jump — count it and make the farthest point the new range end.' },
  136: { q: 'Gas stations sit on a circular route. Station i gives gas[i] and driving to the next costs cost[i]. Starting with an empty tank, find the station from which you can drive all the way around once, or −1 if none. The answer is unique if it exists.',
    a: 'If the total gas is less than the total cost, no start works. Otherwise drive from a candidate start keeping a running tank. If the tank goes negative at station i, no station between the start and i can work either (each would arrive at i with even less), so restart from i + 1 with an empty tank. The last start chosen is the answer.' },
  137: { q: 'Can the cards be split into groups of exactly groupSize cards, where each group is a run of consecutive values (like 3, 4, 5)? Every card must be used.',
    a: 'The smallest card left can’t be in the middle or end of a run — there’s nothing smaller to come before it — so it must start one. Repeatedly take the smallest value, and use all its remaining copies as run starts: each of the next groupSize − 1 values must have at least that many copies. Any shortage means it’s impossible.' },
  138: { q: 'Cut the string into as many parts as possible so that each letter appears in at most one part. Return the sizes of the parts. “ababcbacadefegdehijhklij” gives [9, 7, 8].',
    a: 'A part can’t end until every letter in it has made its last appearance. Record each letter’s last index. As you scan, stretch the current part’s end to the last occurrence of every letter you meet. When the scan reaches that end, nothing inside appears later — cut here, which is the earliest (and so best) place.' },

  // ── Intervals ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  140: { q: 'Build a calendar where book(start, end) adds an event over [start, end) — end not included — only if it doesn’t overlap any existing booking. Return true if it was added, false otherwise.',
    a: 'Two half-open intervals overlap exactly when each one starts before the other ends: s1 < e2 and s2 < e1. For a new booking, check this against every existing one; if none overlap, store it. (A sorted structure makes each check O(log n), but the simple list is fine here.)' },
  141: { q: 'You have a sorted list of non-overlapping intervals and one new interval. Insert it so the list stays sorted and non-overlapping, merging wherever needed, and return the result.',
    a: 'Because the list is sorted, it splits into three parts: intervals entirely before the new one (copy them), intervals that overlap it (merge them into one by taking the smallest start and largest end), and intervals entirely after it (copy them). One pass, O(n), no re-sorting.' },
  142: { q: 'Merge all overlapping intervals and return the merged list. [1,3] and [2,6] overlap and become [1,6]; intervals that only touch, like [1,4] and [4,5], also merge.',
    a: 'Sort by start time so overlapping intervals end up next to each other. Then walk through: if an interval starts before (or at) the end of the last merged one, it overlaps — extend that end with max(), since a short interval may sit entirely inside a long one. Otherwise start a new merged interval.' },
  143: { q: 'Remove the minimum number of intervals so that the rest don’t overlap. Intervals that only touch, like [1,2] and [2,3], don’t count as overlapping.',
    a: 'Flip the question: keep as many intervals as possible. Greedily keep the interval that ENDS earliest — it leaves the most room for the others. Sort by end time and keep each interval that starts after the last kept one ends. Everything not kept has to be removed.' },
  144: { q: 'A car with capacity seats drives east. Trips [passengers, from, to] pick up at from and drop off at to. Can every trip be served without ever going over capacity?',
    a: 'Only the load at each point matters. Record +passengers where a trip starts and −passengers where it ends (they leave at “to”, freeing seats). Sweeping the positions in order with a running sum gives the load everywhere; if it ever exceeds the capacity, it’s impossible. A difference array does this in O(n + range).' },
  145: { q: 'For each query point q, find the size (right − left + 1) of the smallest interval that contains q. If no interval contains it, the answer is −1.',
    a: 'Answer the queries in increasing order. Sort the intervals by start, and for each query, push every interval that has started into a min-heap ordered by size. Throw away heap entries that already ended before q — they can’t contain this or any later query. The heap top is the smallest interval covering q. Put the answers back in the original query order.' },

  // ── Math & Geometry ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  147: { q: 'Repeatedly replace a number with the sum of the squares of its digits. If the process eventually reaches 1, the number is “happy”. Otherwise it loops forever without ever reaching 1. Decide which.',
    a: 'The sequence either reaches 1 or falls into a cycle — the values stay small, so it can’t grow forever. Detect the cycle by remembering every value seen in a set (or with Floyd’s slow/fast pointers). Seeing 1 means happy; seeing a value twice means stuck in a loop.' },
  148: { q: 'Rotate an n × n matrix 90 degrees clockwise, IN PLACE — change the given matrix directly instead of building a new one.',
    a: 'A clockwise rotation equals two simple steps: transpose (swap across the main diagonal, so rows become columns), then reverse each row. Both steps work in place with swaps. In the transpose, only swap cells above the diagonal so each pair is swapped once.' },
  149: { q: 'Return all elements of the matrix in spiral order: along the top row to the right, down the right column, back along the bottom row, up the left column, then the same again further inside.',
    a: 'Keep four boundaries: top, bottom, left, right. Walk each edge in turn and shrink that boundary after you’ve walked it. The only subtlety is single remaining rows or columns: check that rows still remain before walking the bottom edge, and that columns remain before walking the left edge, or you’ll repeat elements.' },
  150: { q: 'If any cell of the matrix is 0, set its entire row and its entire column to 0. Do it in place, ideally with O(1) extra space.',
    a: 'You need to remember which rows and columns to clear, and the matrix’s own first row and first column can store that: a 0 at (r, c) sets matrix[r][0] and matrix[0][c] to 0. Then clear the inner cells using these markers. Since the first row and column double as markers, remember separately whether they themselves started with a 0 and clear them last.' },

  // ── Bit Manipulation ────────────────────────────────────────────
  152: { q: 'Every number in the array appears exactly twice, except one that appears once. Find that one in O(n) time with O(1) extra space.',
    a: 'XOR has three useful properties: x ^ x = 0, x ^ 0 = x, and order doesn’t matter. XOR all the numbers together: each pair cancels itself out, and only the single number is left.' },
  153: { q: 'Count how many bits are set to 1 in the binary form of an unsigned 32-bit integer (its “Hamming weight”). 11 is 1011 in binary, so the answer is 3.',
    a: 'n & (n − 1) removes the lowest 1-bit from n: subtracting 1 flips that bit and every 0 below it, and the AND clears them. Repeat until n becomes 0 and count the steps — that’s one step per set bit instead of always checking all 32.' },
  154: { q: 'For every number from 0 to n, count the 1-bits in its binary form, and return all n + 1 counts in a list. Aim for O(n), not a separate count for each number.',
    a: 'Shifting i right by one (i >> 1) drops its last bit, giving a smaller number whose count is already known. Add that dropped bit back (i & 1): bits[i] = bits[i >> 1] + (i & 1). Each answer is built from an earlier one in O(1).' },
  155: { q: 'Reverse the order of the 32 bits of an unsigned integer: bit 0 becomes bit 31, bit 1 becomes bit 30, and so on.',
    a: 'Build the answer one bit at a time. Exactly 32 times: shift the result left to make room, copy n’s lowest bit into it, then shift n right. The first bit taken from n has been shifted all the way up to the top by the end, so the order comes out reversed.' },
  156: { q: 'The array contains n distinct numbers taken from 0 to n, so exactly one number in that range is missing. Find it in O(n) time with O(1) extra space.',
    a: 'Every number from 0 to n is present except one. XOR all the indices 0..n together with all the values: each number that’s present cancels with its matching index, leaving only the missing number. (The sum formula n(n + 1)/2 minus the array’s sum works too, but XOR can’t overflow.)' },
};
